@region lead text
H161 Wireless from Olympic’s Captain Gives News
H167 and Weeping Women Gather at White
C Star Offices to Learn Fate of Relatives—1,323
H157 Passengers on Ship
C and Crew of 890.
H158 SURVIVORS EIGHT HOURS IN BOATS
  No Word from Virginian or Parisian, Which Vice-President
C Franklin Hopes Saved Others—Disaster Greatest in
C History, if Hope Is Unfounded—Captain
C Believed to Have Gone Down
C at His Post of Duty.
L FIRST CLASS PASSENGERS ON TITANIC | 318
L SECOND CLASS PASSENGERS ON TITANIC | 261
L THIRD CLASS PASSENGERS ON TITANIC | 740
L CREW OF THE TITANIC | 890
L TOTAL | 2,209
L REPORTED SAVED ON THE CARPATHIA | 866
L MISSING | 1,343
P The White Star liner Titanic sank at 2:20 a. m. yesterday about
  1,150 miles east of Sandy Hook.
P First reports placed the number of survivors, passengers and crew,
  at 675. At 2:30 o’clock this morning a dispatch relayed by the Olympic
  said the number aboard the Carpathia was 866.
P A still later dispatch said that the Allan Line steamer Virginian had
  probably picked up some of the victims.
P P. A. S. Franklin, vice-president of the International Mercantile
  Marine Company, admitted last night that probably only 675 out of
  more than 2,200 souls on the lost vessel were saved, which means that
  more than 1,500 persons went down with her.
P When the full text of the Olympic’s message was given out it was
  found that it estimated the loss at 1,800.
P Shortly before 11 o’clock last night a slight ray of hope that more
  than the 675 on the Carpathia had been saved appeared in a message
  from the Marconi operator at Sable Island, near the scene of the dis-
  aster.
P In answer to an inquiry regarding the delivery of wireless mes-
  sages to passengers on the Titanic, the operator at Sable Island replied
  that it was difficult to deliver them, “as the passengers are believed to
  ⟦’I⟧ be dispersed among several vessels.”
P Those saved are coming to New York on the Cunard liner Car-
  pathia.
P The Carpathia is expected to reach New York on Thursday night.
  Mr. Franklin sent a wireless message to her last night asking that the
  ⟦T⟧ names of the survivors aboard be sent by wireless.
P A report that John Jacob Astor was among those lost and that
  Mrs. Astor had been saved was current last night, but could not be
  confirmed.
C MOSTLY WOMEN AND CHILDREN SAVED.
P Both passengers and crew are among those saved. Of the passen-
  gers saved nearly all are women and children.
P The only report received is from the Carpathia, via the Olympic.
  The Virginian and the Parisian, which were also racing to the founder-
  ing Titanic, have not yet been heard from.
P The Olympic, of the White Star Line, reported that the Carpathia
  was the first vessel to reach the position of the Titanic.
P She found only small boats and wreckage.
P All the boats of the Titanic were accounted for, according to the
  Olympic.
P Vice-President Franklin said shortly after 11 o’clock last night
  that he had received a wireless message saying that the Carpathia had
  rescued the survivors of the Titanic she carried from a small fleet of
  lifeboats picked up at 10:30 o’clock yesterday morning, more than eight
  hours after the Titanic sank.
P He said that he believed the Carpathia was making all possible
  speed for New York in order that the rescued passengers, who were
  suffering from exposure, might have medical attention at the earliest
  minute.
P The Leyland liner Californian remained in the vicinity of the
  disaster to search for survivors.
P Captain E. J. Smith, commodore of the White Star fleet, is believed
  to have gone down with his ship.
P Among those aboard the ship were F. D. Millet, the artist and
  president of the Consolidated American Academy at Rome; Mr. and
  Mrs. John Jacob Astor, Major Archibald Butt, military aid to President
  Taft; C. M. Hays, president of the Grand Trunk Railway; J. Bruce
  Ismay, chairman and managing director of the White Star Line; W. T.
  Stead, Mrs. Isidor Straus, Mr. and Mrs. G. D. Widener, Mr. and Mrs.
  Harry Widener, Benjamin Guggenheim, Countess Rothes, Mr. and
  Mrs. D. W. Marvin, who were returning from their wedding trip, the
  latter a sister of Mrs. Wheelock, of No. 317 Riverside Drive; Norman
  Carlyle Craig, M. P. for the Isle of Thanet; Mr. and Mrs. Washington
  Dodge, Colonel Archibald Gracie and Mr. and Mrs. Henry S. Harper.
  WEEPING WOMEN GATHER AT WHITE STAR OFFICES.
P Shortly after 9 o’clock last night weeping women began to gather
  at the White Star offices, at No. 9 Broadway, seeking news of relatives
  who had sailed on the Titanic.
P The first statement given out at the White Star offices conceding
  the loss of the Titanic said:
P “Captain Haddock of the Olympic sends a wireless message to
  the White Star offices here that the steamer Titanic sank at 2:20 a. m.,
  after all passengers and crew had been lowered to lifeboats and trans-
