H151 HOW TITANIC’S CAREER
H139 WAS ENDED BY ICEBERG
P While the new ⟦ten-milllon-dollar⟧ White Star liner Titanic, carrying to this
  port some of the richest and most prominent persons in the ⟦I'nited⟧ States and
  ⟦fj⟧ England, was steaming along on her mal trip she crashed Into a fog
  i! hidden iceberg, to the eastward of Cape Race, at 10:25 o'clock on Sunday night,
  and four hours later she went to the bottom of the Atlantic.
P Fresh from the builders’ yards, the biggest merchantman in the world left
  l last 1ast Wednesday with a passenger complement of 1,470 ⟦sgouls⟧ and
  “a crew of 890, and from the speed sh® had been making with her forced draft
  ⟦‘l⟧ and picked coal it was certain she would have reached this port in record time.
P Nothing thus far has heen learned to indicate her speed at the time of tho
  H collision, but from the impact with the ice mountain that sent her to the hot
  torn it la almost certain she was plunging along at close to 21 knots, tha beast Pl
  ‘velocity her triple screws could give.
C More Than 1,500 Persons Believed to Have Perished.
P Only 675 souls of tha 2,360 on board have thus far been accounted for, and
  it is believed more than 1500 persons have perished with the biggest ship in
  ‘the world, which was thought to be unsinkable,
P Misfortune and mishap had camped in the wake of the Titanic's sister ship,
  ⟦x‘⟧ {he Olympic, and it was hoped by the White Star Line that the latest leviathan
  ‘from the Harland Wolff yards, at Belfast, would have better luck.
P Fate. however, seemed to have Set Its seal on the big. ⟦unwisldy⟧ queens of
  the Atlantic, and the Titanic did not escape. Three times had the ⟦Olympie⟧
  'been in trouble, tha mishaps costing her owners almost a million dollars for
  ⟦drvdocking,⟧ logs of business and repairs.
P Only once did the Titanic get in trouble. That was off Cape Race on Sun-
  ‘day night, when she went down in water go deep that no means known (o man
  H could bring her back to the ocean highway.
P Never in the history of shipping has a vessel of her great cost and ⟦tonnaga⟧
  ⟦émnod⟧ the seas, much ⟦lezs⟧ been wiped out of existence so quickly and so un-
  expectedly.
P She was looked upon by the shipping world as the unsinkable ship. Her
  ⟦ihnndon⟧ have boasted that no seas could harm her nor could any vessel afloat
  damage her to the point of =inking. All these apparently substantial ⟦conten-⟧
  ⟦{ions⟧ were made with no reference to treacherous fogs or deadly ⟦jcebergs,⟧ the
